---
title: Change Cache Location
description: Learn how to customize where Nx stores its cache files by modifying the cacheDirectory setting in your nx.json configuration.
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Tasks & caching'
---

Nx stores the cache in a per-user directory outside your workspace, `~/.nx/<hash>/cache`, where the hash identifies the checkout. A checkout and every worktree added to it share one directory, so a task built in one worktree is a cache hit in the others. A second clone of the same repository is a different checkout, and gets its own. Cache results are stored for a week before they get deleted.

You can customize the location in the `nx.json` file:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "cacheDirectory": "/tmp/mycache"
}
```

## Why it is outside the workspace

Each checkout gets one directory, `~/.nx/<hash>`, holding both `cache` and `workspace-data`.

Worktrees share it. A main checkout and every worktree added to it resolve to the same directory, so none of them rebuilds what another already has.

An AI agent sandbox can reach it. A sandbox grants filesystem paths, and `~/.nx` is spelled the same on every machine, so the grant can live in a settings file you commit. An absolute checkout path cannot. `nx configure-ai-agents` writes that grant into `.claude/settings.json` for Claude Code.

{% aside type="note" title="If you cache `.nx/cache` in CI" %}
Nx no longer writes there by default. Point your CI cache step at `~/.nx` instead, or set `NX_CACHE_DIRECTORY` to keep the old location.
{% /aside %}

## Opting out

Setting `cacheDirectory` opts out of the shared location. Worktrees configuring the same value still share one cache, through the main checkout. Worktrees configuring different values each keep their own. `NX_CACHE_DIRECTORY`, `NX_WORKSPACE_DATA_DIRECTORY` and `NX_PROJECT_GRAPH_CACHE_DIRECTORY` opt out the same way.

## When Nx falls back

When Nx cannot make `~/.nx` owner-only, it uses the **main checkout's** `.nx` directories, which is where worktrees shared before this directory existed. A `sudo nx` run that kept your `HOME` is the usual cause, since it leaves the directory owned by root. In a main checkout that is its own `.nx`; in a worktree it is the main checkout's, not the worktree's.

A process that cannot write under `~/.nx` at all gets a cache in **its own** checkout. Nx establishes this by writing a marker file rather than by recognizing the caller, so a sandbox Nx has never heard of is covered as long as it denies the write. A sandbox that permits writes to `~/.nx` is not falling back, and does not need to.

`nx reset` clears both the shared directory for the current repository and the checkout's own. Nothing reclaims the directory belonging to a checkout you have since deleted or moved, so remove those by hand.
